+### 8.4a Metric planning after response completion (`ext1-3`)
+
+`metric_set_id` is deliberately unknown until response inventory and Class-C
+reference resolution exist. A second supported Snakemake checkpoint,
+`plan_metric_set`, bridges this boundary within `simulate_system.smk`:
+
+1. At parse time compute a `metric_request_id` from `simulation_id`, the
+   selected declarations, grouping/reference semantics and code digests,
+   stage-3 environment and §7.5 validation declaration/report digest. Exclude
+   unavailable response-inventory digest and resolved reference provenance.
+   This is a scheduling key, never a scientific result identity.
+2. The checkpoint depends on completed `response_inventory.json`, its declared
+   native artifacts, and the immutable request/declaration/environment inputs.
+   Native output paths are enumerated from the stage-2 run/request contract;
+   their bytes and reference months are not read before their producers finish.
+   In simulation mode the response producers are present; in metrics-only they
+   are external validated leaves and no simulation producer is defined.
+3. At execution it verifies complete retained responses, resolves Class-C
+   references exactly as §7.2, creates bundle memberships/expected result keys,
+   and computes §8.4's final identity. It atomically writes
+   `<exp>/results/metric_plans/<metric_request_id>/plan.json`, schema
+   `metric-plan/1`, containing request id/payload, actual response digest,
+   resolved reference provenance, expected unit/key sets, environment and
+   validation descriptors, `metric_set_id`, exact target paths and canonical
+   `plan_sha256` excluding itself. No metric value is written by this checkpoint.
+4. The `all`/`metrics` target input function calls this checkpoint's public
+   `.get()`, reads the completed plan and returns the exact `metrics.json`
+   target. Reduction and publication rules use constrained `metric_set_id`
+   wildcards, depend on the plan, and reject any id not equal to its resolved id.
+   Indicator files and manifest remain declared DAG outputs, not hidden
+   runtime side effects. The final publisher checks all §7.4 invariants and
+   writes `metrics.json` last; the plan is never a ready-result marker.
+
+Plans are rebuildable and excluded from scientific identities: all result-
+affecting plan contents already enter the final identity. Native artifact bytes
+are reverified before planning/reduction and on every invocation's existing-plan
+preflight, even for a no-job DAG. Existing plans are reusable only after checking
+their request and response digests. A stale plan is rebuilt or
+refused, never used to select an older result. No new checkpoint platform,
+execution-attempt record, global lock or resume guarantee is introduced.
+
+In metrics-only mode all determining responses must exist, so the same planner
+can compute the expected identity read-only at parse time to validate a direct
+metric filename. Only the selected set's declared output filenames are allowed;
+the execution checkpoint still validates and materializes its plan. Unknown or
+mismatched filenames refuse. In fresh simulation mode the supported target is
+`all`; its final identity is resolved after stage 2 without a second invocation.
+Success reports exact collection id/path and metric-set id/path; the runner
+reads the selected plans and validates those exact ready manifests. No `latest`
+pointer, directory scan or fallback to a retained set is allowed. A fresh
+dry-run reports that metric identity awaits responses instead of inventing it.
+GF-30 must demonstrate this entire transition from absent responses.
+For both checkpoints, final ready-artifact reuse must occur before any scheduled
+rule can remove published files. Use the supported output-preservation mechanism
+(`update(...)` where a revalidation rule retains an existing output), never a
+`directory()` rule over an existing immutable collection/result set. Forced-rerun
+fixtures must prove that unchanged outputs survive and mismatches refuse.
